      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The radius of a nucleus,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;is related to its atomic&nbsp;number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;as:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup></math></p><p>where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is the nuclear radius of hydrogen.</p><p>Thus, the density of a nucleus is given by:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></msup></mrow></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#956;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mtext>Constant</mtext></math>&nbsp;</p><p>(Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#956;</mi></math>&nbsp;is atomic mass unit)</p><p>Hence, the ratio of nuclear density of oxygen to helium nucleus is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>"
  }
}